  {
   "id": "m3-23",
   "name": "Transpose, determinant and adjoint of a matrix"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "• The transpose Aᵀ turns rows into columns: the first row of A becomes the first column of Aᵀ. A 2 × 3 matrix has a 3 × 2 transpose.\n• For A = (a b ; c d), the determinant is |A| = ad − bc (leading diagonal product minus the other diagonal product).\n• A matrix with determinant 0 is singular; otherwise it is non-singular.\n• The adjoint of (a b ; c d) is (d −b ; −c a): swap the elements of the leading diagonal and change the signs of the other two.\n• A × adj A = |A| × I.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "learn": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "sections": [
   {
    "heading": "The transpose",
    "text": "Write the rows of A as columns and you get its transpose, Aᵀ. A {2} × {3} matrix becomes {3} × {2}:\nA = {matStr([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])} gives Aᵀ = {matStr([[1, 4], [2, 5], [3, 6]])}.\nThe element in row i, column j of A moves to row j, column i."
   },
   {
    "heading": "The determinant",
    "text": "Every square matrix has a number called its determinant. For a {2} × {2} matrix A = (a b ; c d):\n|A| = ad − bc: the product of the leading diagonal minus the product of the other diagonal.\n|{matStr([[3, 1], [4, 2]])}| = {3} × {2} − {1} × {4} = {2}.\nIf |A| = {0}, A is called singular. You will see next lesson that a singular matrix has no inverse."
   },
   {
    "heading": "The adjoint",
    "text": "The adjoint of A = (a b ; c d) is adj A = (d −b ; −c a): swap a and d, and change the signs of b and c.\nadj {matStr([[3, 1], [4, 2]])} = {matStr([[2, -1], [-4, 3]])}.\nCheck: A × adj A = {matStr(matMul([[3, 1], [4, 2]], [[2, -1], [-4, 3]]))} = |A| × I. This fact gives the inverse."
   }
  ],
  "key_words": [
   {
    "word": "transpose",
    "meaning": "Aᵀ: rows written as columns"
   },
   {
    "word": "determinant",
    "meaning": "|A| = ad − bc for a {2} × {2} matrix"
   },
   {
    "word": "singular",
    "meaning": "having determinant {0}"
   },
   {
    "word": "adjoint",
    "meaning": "adj A = (d −b ; −c a)"
   }
  ],
  "examples": [
   "|{matStr([[5, 2], [3, 1]])}| = {5} − {6} = {-1}",
   "adj {matStr([[1, 4], [2, 7]])} = {matStr([[7, -4], [-2, 1]])}"
  ],
  "mistake": {
   "wrong": "|{matStr([[3, 1], [4, 2]])}| = {3} × {2} + {1} × {4} = {10}",
   "right": "|A| = {3} × {2} − {1} × {4} = {2}",
   "why": "The determinant is a difference: ad minus bc."
  }
 },
 "worked_example": {
